  "solution": "Let the roots of  \n\\[\nf(x)=30x^3-50x^2+22x-1\n\\]\nbe \\(p,q,r\\). Then\n\\[\nf(x)=30(x-p)(x-q)(x-r).\n\\]\n\nWe want\n\\[\nS=\\frac1{1-p}+\\frac1{1-q}+\\frac1{1-r}.\n\\]\n\nNotice that\n\\[\nS=\\frac{(1-q)(1-r)+(1-p)(1-r)+(1-p)(1-q)}{(1-p)(1-q)(1-r)}.\n\\]\n\nNow compute \\(f(1)\\) and \\(f'(1)\\):\n\\[\nf(1)=30-50+22-1=1.\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\nf'(x)=90x^2-100x+22,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nf'(1)=90-100+22=12.\n\\]\n\nUsing\n\\[\nf(x)=30(x-p)(x-q)(x-r),\n\\]\nwe have\n\\[\nf(1)=30(1-p)(1-q)(1-r)\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nf'(1)=30\\big[(1-q)(1-r)+(1-p)(1-r)+(1-p)(1-q)\\big].\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\nS=\\frac{f'(1)}{f(1)}=\\frac{12}{1}=12.\n\\]\n\nThe answer is 12.",
